  'acquittal-ruling':{word:'Acquittal',definition:'A court’s finding that a defendant is not guilty of the charged offence because guilt has not been established under the applicable standard. An acquittal is not necessarily a declaration that the person is factually innocent; appeal rules vary.',synonyms:['not-guilty verdict','finding of not guilty','acquittal decision'],example:{word:'acquittal',text:'The jury returned an acquittal after finding that the prosecution had not proved the charge.'},exampleSynonym:{word:'not-guilty verdict',text:'The jury returned a not-guilty verdict after finding that the prosecution had not proved the charge.'}},
  'adversarial-system':{definition:'A legal procedure in which opposing parties present evidence and arguments to a neutral decision-maker, who resolves the dispute. The judge may have case-management or evidentiary duties, and systems can combine adversarial and inquisitorial features.',synonyms:['adversary system','party-led judicial process'],example:{word:'adversarial system',text:'In an adversarial system, the parties present their cases while the judge acts as an impartial decision-maker.'},exampleSynonym:{word:'adversary system',text:'In an adversary system, the parties present their cases while the judge acts as an impartial decision-maker.'}}
